SHA256 140c4db6c85f7f606533324fa1f3b7d4f8e9922ba6b2c8189c380cd8e8086558
Implement one dormant connection from an authenticated five-file namespace to the fixed owned WhisperX constructor. Use the repaired owner transaction as the base. Preserve the generated route, public real-entry refusals, accepted tests and rejected constructor01 unchanged. This brief proposes source work only; it reports no new qualification result.

The gap is specific. `InheritedReadSetAdoption` validates hardcoded generated sizes and digests, so its success cannot authorize real model buffers. `GenerationChannel` authenticates messages using the supplied key but does not establish that key's provenance. `WorkerBootstrap.build_factory_product` requires its issued generated namespace. Owned WhisperX then calls `WhisperModel` without `files`, while B3 already supports a buffer constructor. Generated ownership qualification covers none of these missing real connections.

SOURCE-INPUTS.json gives exact existing paths, byte hashes and read coverage. In the edits below, N means `_scratch/windows-interrupted-owner-native-proposal02`, R means `_scratch/protected-engine-ownership-repair02`, and W means `_scratch/whisperx-owned-derivative-proposal01/after/whisperx`. These are existing source directories, not installed modules. Place derivatives, before copies, mechanical diffs and new tests in a fresh implementation directory selected by root.

1. Derive N/inherited_readset.py and N/pinned_buffer_namespace.py for a separate private admitted-model route. Reuse the existing same-channel child ordering at N/generated_adapter_flow.py:1331–1363. Controller bootstrap retains the selected `TrustedManifest` model, its external approval, exact `RuntimeProfile` and live admission/permit. Its private inherited channel binds that approved passive selection/profile to the child generation and process; the child issues a distinct local namespace lease from this trusted start. The controller permit stays local and is never serialized. A digest, reconstructed dataclass, caller path or supplied files dictionary grants no authority. Compare the authenticated adoption payload to the selected model's full membership and retained physical identities, including distinct inherited handles, before reading content. Bind generation, process creation, manifest and namespace to the same bootstrap. Keep the old generated validator intact; do not replace its fixed hashes with caller assertions. Retain the adoption and materialization owner before fallible work, and retain verified buffers through constructor failure. Read model content only after `begin`; an initial cancel allocates no model buffers or factory product.

2. Derive R/worker_runtime_owner.py and R/owned_generation_protocol.py. Add private issuance of an exact owner-held namespace lease after successful selected-member readback. Bind its read set, immutable buffers, manifest/model, profile and generation by identity. Add a separate fixed factory entry requiring that lease before any factory activity; preserve the existing generated entry's unconditional namespace check. Reuse N/owned_factory_port.py and N/model_binding_registry.py for the exact factory/product/model lease, requiring the current owned module's `_RUNTIME` to be this registry. Missing real state/recipe/tensor authority remains a refusal. Never call `issue_generated_inputs` to authorize real buffers. Register the actual VAD product before engine construction, and keep its exact lease live through publication.

3. Add the private fixed engine connection proposed by the original plan (`owned_asr_engine.py`) and a private constructor entry in a derivative of W/asr.py. Bind the actual owned module and its exact `WhisperModel`, `Tokenizer`, `TranscriptionOptions`, `FasterWhisperPipeline` and `VoiceActivitySegmentation` classes in trusted bootstrap; accept no constructor callback or replacement runtime from callers or IPC. Use one owner operation. Retain the attempt before construction and each returned model, tokenizer and pipeline before the next fallible step or liveness check. Publish only with current namespace/profile/generation/VAD lease and exact pipeline links. The real pipeline field is `vad_model`; the generated `.vad` and `.segmentation` fixture checks are not real object contracts. Preserve the first error and all uncertain references. A native allocation hidden by a constructor that never returns remains a worker-retirement obligation.

4. Forward a fresh dictionary of the five verified buffers into the existing B3 `files` parameter. B3 pops tokenizer/preprocessor entries; the retained immutable namespace must survive that mutation. Require nonempty approved tokenizer and preprocessor bytes before entry. Keep four-file models refused: absent or empty preprocessor bytes reach B3's filesystem fallback. No companion edit or fabricated preprocessor asset is needed for this first unit. The absolute snapshot identity required by W/_uoink_owned.py remains bound to the admitted namespace; it is not permission to reopen paths. Bind the existing CPU `RuntimeProfile` to an explicit private constructor/options record: exact compute mode, device index, positive thread/worker limits, language/task and all supported options. Missing values refuse instead of selecting `auto` or library defaults. The facade's beam/best-of constraint remains one. Keep public `load_model`, `assert_load_parameters` and activation refusals unchanged; the new private method validates its own issued attempt.

5. Add only the private bootstrap call sequence needed to reach namespace issuance, fixed VAD registration and construction. Preserve N/generated_worker_runtime_bridge.py and the ordinary generated child route as historical behavior. A real constructor result stays worker-local. Do not treat `release_after_reads` as backend retirement or add a clean-close success based on Python reference dropping. PCM, filters, inference/cursor conversion, completion metadata and caller migration remain subsequent connections.

Use the existing guarded fake loader and fixed bootstrap/factory fixtures. Add focused inert controls that enter the actual new methods: authenticated selection/handle/member mismatch and copied-lease refusal before construction; pre-begin cancel without materialization; successful five-file forwarding with fixed class/VAD/profile identity; four-file, empty preprocessor and missing tokenizer refusal before any path service; dictionary mutation with retained immutable buffers; rejection of missing/changed policy; namespace or VAD revocation during construction with the returned object retained; tokenizer and pipeline-constructor failures after model return; final publication refusal after revocation; secondary cleanup failure preserving the original error; and overlap/nested operation refusal. Exercise the captured B3 constructor branch through inert lower services so the test does not bypass the new private WhisperX entry. Preserve existing generated, owned WhisperX and B3 assertions, adapting their existing source loader only where needed. Do not predeclare a passing count or add a new guard framework.

No Ryan-only decision blocks this source preparation. Real use still requires an externally approved complete selected `TrustedManifest` and explicit runtime profile, including the real VAD state/recipe and native reader/import closure. Current `REAL_APPROVAL`, `RELEASE_AUTHORITY` and `RUNTIME_PROFILE` remain absent; this proposal must not populate them or manufacture approval. The original plan records missing auxiliary hashes and separate D3 acquisition/D4 runtime decisions. D1/D2 stay complete with no reread. No native execution, model/support access, production activation, shared-doc change or commit is authorized here.
